  "summary_md": "Housing and discretionary-spending headwinds persist across the furnishings batch, but the week’s dominant thread is strategic repositioning—through M&A, radical business pivots, and restructuring. **AMWD** cleared an FTC review for its MasterBrand merger, setting up a consolidation play. **LCUT** and **COOK** stand out as tangible turnaround stories, with proactive pricing, cost discipline, and product innovation translating into earnings beats despite soft demand. At the far edge of repositioning, **ATER** has exited its legacy e-commerce business entirely to trade critical minerals, while **XWIN** and **EFOI** are pivoting into AI and energy infrastructure respectively, supported by early pipeline and partnership announcements but limited independent commentary.\n\nOn the stressed side, **SNBR** faces a severe demand downturn and is exploring capital-structure options without providing guidance, mirroring **PRPL**’s transparency and credibility concerns. **HOFT** and **KEQU** are executing restructurings and acquisition integrations with mixed near-term visibility. Several smaller names (**CRWS**, **VIRC**) offer virtually no fundamental narrative, while the **NCL** commentary appears disconnected from its furnishings mandate—an anomaly readers will spot quickly.",
